# Comparison of regularization strategies

All parameters were chosen from the data alone (noise level sigma_hat = 0.02887
estimated from the tail of the Fourier spectrum; effective rank k_eff = 24).

## Results summary

| method | parameter | rule | residual ||y-Kx|| | vs delta = 0.43022 | ||x|| | head energy (i<=24) | tail energy (i>24) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| A Tikhonov | alpha = 3.272002e-03 | discrepancy principle (known noise level) | 0.43022 | 1.000 | 3.5559 | 3.4948 | 6.565e-01 |
| B TSVS | k = 24 | s_i > sigma_hat cutoff | 0.41715 | 0.970 | 5.1595 | 5.1595 | 4.048e-15 |
| C CGLS | k = 24 | effective-rank fallback (see below) | 1.81860 | 4.227 | 0.1866 | 0.1866 | 2.757e-04 |

Cross-checks for the Tikhonov parameter (data only): GCV minimum at
alpha = 4.038e-04; the L-curve (see tikhonov/lcurve_data.csv) is smooth with
no sharp knee in this regime.

## Fit to the data
- Tikhonov fits the data exactly to the estimated noise level
  (residual = delta = sigma_hat*sqrt(n)): the residual is consistent with pure
  white noise, i.e. nothing below the noise floor is fitted.
- TSVS k = 24 fits the first 24 Fourier coefficients exactly; its residual
  (0.4172) is at the tail-noise level (sqrt(sum yhat_i^2, i>24)).
- CGLS fits far less (residual 1.8186 >> delta): the iteration STALLS.
  On this K, K^T K has condition number ~3.3e+29, so CGLS in double
  precision loses Krylov orthogonality and stalls: residual
  1.8186 (k=24), 1.7735 (k=100),
  1.7733 (k=222 = full convergence limit in exact arithmetic).
  After 222 iterations it has captured only ~14% of the leading Fourier
  coefficient. CGLS is therefore not competitive on this problem.

